\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 16. Your component API matters more than your backend\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 This is the biggest change from my previous answer.\
Your main engineering challenge isn't:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 "How do I build authentication?"\
It's:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 "How do I design component APIs that remain useful across dozens of applications?"
\f0\b0 \
For example, this is bad:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
<LoginPage\
  authProvider="better-auth"\
/>\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 because you've coupled your UI to a technology.\
This is much better:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
<LoginPage\
  providers=\{["google", "github"]\}\
  onSubmit=\{handleLogin\}\
  onProviderLogin=\{handleProviderLogin\}\
/>\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 Now the consumer owns the implementation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 17. Design components around states\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 For every reusable component, think:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
                 COMPONENT\
                     
\f3 \uc0\u9474 
\f2 \
       
\f3 \uc0\u9484 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9532 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9488 
\f2 \
       
\f3 \uc0\u9660 
\f2              
\f3 \uc0\u9660 
\f2              
\f3 \uc0\u9660 
\f2 \
    Data          Actions        State\
       
\f3 \uc0\u9474 
\f2              
\f3 \uc0\u9474 
\f2              
\f3 \uc0\u9474 
\f2 \
       
\f3 \uc0\u9660 
\f2              
\f3 \uc0\u9660 
\f2              
\f3 \uc0\u9660 
\f2 \
    props        callbacks     loading\
                                error\
                                empty\
                                disabled\
                                success\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
<ApiKeyManager\
  keys=\{keys\}\
  loading=\{loading\}\
  error=\{error\}\
  onCreate=\{createKey\}\
  onRevoke=\{revokeKey\}\
/>\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 That makes it extraordinarily reusable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 18. Use controlled components for important behavior\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 For reusable components, prefer APIs such as:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
<Dialog\
  open=\{open\}\
  onOpenChange=\{setOpen\}\
>\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 rather than:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
<Dialog />\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 Likewise:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
<Tabs\
  value=\{activeTab\}\
  onValueChange=\{setActiveTab\}\
/>\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 This gives the consuming application control.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 19. But don't make every component painfully configurable\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 There is an opposite failure mode.\
You don't want:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
<Button\
  padding="..."\
  borderRadius="..."\
  fontSize="..."\
  shadow="..."\
  color="..."\
  hoverColor="..."\
  ...\
/>\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 That's basically rebuilding CSS through props.\
Instead:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
<Button variant="primary" size="lg">\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 and allow normal 
\f2\fs26 className
\f0\fs24 /styling escape hatches.\
Your theme handles the rest.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 20. The package should feel like this\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 A future project:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
pnpm add @yourname/ui @yourname/pages\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 Then:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
import \{ Button, Card, Dialog \} from "@yourname/ui";\
\
import \{\
  LoginPage,\
  SettingsLayout,\
  BillingPage\
\} from "@yourname/pages";\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 And:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
export default function Login() \{\
  return (\
    <LoginPage\
      providers=\{["google", "github"]\}\
